# Trivy ignore list for the security-trivy CI job (R-12).
#
# Format: one vulnerability ID per line (CVE-…, GHSA-…), optionally followed
# by `exp:YYYY-MM-DD` to expire the entry. Put the reason, the owner and the
# affected image(s) in a comment on the line above, e.g.
#   # libfoo in python:3.12-slim, not reachable from the worker; owner <name>
#   CVE-2026-00000 exp:2027-01-31
#
# Prefer rebuilding on a patched base image or bumping the dependency; an
# entry here is an accepted risk that someone signs for. Keep it short:
# every other fixable HIGH/CRITICAL finding fails CI.

# CVE-2025-46569 (GHSA-6m8w-jc87-6cr7), github.com/open-policy-agent/opa
# v0.68.0, fixed only in 1.4.0 (the Rego-v1 breaking major; bumping is a
# separate migration). The bug is Rego injection through the request path of
# OPA's *standalone server* REST Data API (/v1/data/{path}); the advisory
# says library users are not affected. We embed OPA as a Go library only:
# services/policy/internal/opa (ast + rego + storage/inmem, fixed query
# "data.vaultdms.authz") and services/workflow/internal/activities
# (rego, fixed query "data.workflow.result"). `go list -deps` of the policy
# and workflow binaries links no opa/server, opa/runtime, opa/cmd or opa/sdk
# package, and no image, compose file or chart runs `opa run --server`.
# Revisit with the OPA 1.x migration. Owner: policy service.
# Affected images: policy, workflow.
CVE-2025-46569 exp:2027-03-31

